'''Kelly Ann Ayotte''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|eɪ|ɒ|t}} {{Respell|AY|ott}};<ref>{{cite web |last=Bowers |first=Andy |author-link=Andy Bowers |title=Plessy, Miranda, Roe, and Ayotte? |url=https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2005/11/how-to-pronounce-kelly-ayotte.html |work=[[Slate (magazine)|Slate]] |access-date=January 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211019015249/https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2005/11/how-to-pronounce-kelly-ayotte.html |archive-date=October 19, 2021 |date=November 30, 2005 |url-status=live}}</ref> born June 27, 1968) is an American attorney and politician serving since 2025 as the 83rd [[governor of New Hampshire]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], she served from 2011 to 2017 as a [[United States Senate|United States senator]] from [[New Hampshire]] and from 2004 to 2009 as the 27th [[Attorney General of New Hampshire|attorney general of New Hampshire]].

Born in [[Nashua, New Hampshire]], Ayotte is a graduate of [[Pennsylvania State University]], and [[Villanova University School of Law]]. She worked as a law clerk at the [[New Hampshire Supreme Court]] before entering private practice. She served as a prosecutor for the [[New Hampshire Department of Justice]] and briefly as the legal counsel to Governor [[Craig Benson]] before returning to the New Hampshire Department of Justice to serve as deputy attorney general. In 2004, Benson appointed Ayotte as attorney general of New Hampshire after [[Peter Heed]] resigned. She became the first woman to serve as New Hampshire's attorney general. She was twice reappointed by Governor [[John Lynch (New Hampshire governor)|John Lynch]]. Ayotte resigned as attorney general in 2009 to run for the U.S. Senate after Senator [[Judd Gregg]] announced his retirement.

In September 2010, Ayotte narrowly defeated lawyer [[Ovide Lamontagne]] in the Republican primary for the U.S. Senate. She then defeated Democratic congressman [[Paul Hodes]] in the [[2010 United States Senate election in New Hampshire|general election]] with 60% of the vote. Ayotte was mentioned as a possible running mate for Republican nominee [[Mitt Romney]] in the [[2012 United States presidential election|2012 presidential election]]. In [[2016 United States Senate election in New Hampshire|2016]], Democratic Governor [[Maggie Hassan]] defeated Ayotte in her reelection bid by 1,017 votes (0.14%).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://sos.nh.gov/2016GenResults.aspx |title=2016 General Election Information and Results |publisher=New Hampshire Secretary of State Elections Division |date=November 8, 2016 |access-date=February 20, 2017}}</ref> After President [[Donald Trump]] nominated Judge [[Neil Gorsuch]] to the [[United States Supreme Court]], the Trump administration chose Ayotte to be the [[Sherpa (political consultant)|sherpa]] in charge of the White House team for [[Neil Gorsuch Supreme Court nomination|Gorsuch's confirmation]].

Ayotte was elected governor of New Hampshire in [[2024 New Hampshire gubernatorial election|2024]], defeating Democratic nominee [[Joyce Craig]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-11-05 |title=Republican ex-Sen. Kelly Ayotte beats Democrat Joyce Craig in New Hampshire governor's race |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-hampshire-governor-election-2024-ayotte-craig-74985b040f72599fb3569bd363e84fa3 |access-date=2024-11-08 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> 

